{"format_version":1,"partition":12145,"records":{"arsenit":{"entries":[["arsenit",null,null,null,{"g":"n","decl":"|||s|e|e|en|e"}]],"senses":[[242560,"arsenit","noun","arsenite",0,"n",null,null,null,null,["inorganic-chemistry"]]]},"berlin":{"entries":[["berlin",null,"[bɛʁˈliːn]","The place is first mentioned by this name in the late 12th century (as Middle Low German Berlyn).\nThe origin is unknown. Slavic by the suffix -in.\nAccording to (incorrect) folk etymology, the name is derived from the Bär (“bear”) that is the symbol of the city.\nIn a popular etymology of Germanists, it is derived from a Polabian stem *berl- ~ *birl- (“swamp”), for which lexical item there is no evidence in Polabian nor in any Slavic language. Closest to that would be an unknown simpler relative of *bьrlogъ (“cave”).\nAttested vocabulary is Polabian poro (“swamp, bog”). *bělь also meant a “swampy meadow” or “white field”, which fits the location at the märkische Streusandbüchse, the “Margraviate pounce box” notorious for its sandy soil, and is also the noun behind Belitz."]],"senses":[[1700,"berlin","name","Berlin (the capital and largest city and state of Germany)",0],[1701,"berlin","name","Berlin (federal government of Germany)",1]],"related_forms":[[1731,"berlin","alt-berlin","derived"],[1732,"berlin","berlin-dahlem","derived"],[1733,"berlin","berlin-friedrichshain","derived"],[1734,"berlin","berlin-johannisthal","derived"],[1735,"berlin","berlin-köpenik","derived"],[1736,"berlin","berlin-lichtenberg","derived"],[1737,"berlin","berlin-marzahn","derived"],[1738,"berlin","berlin-mitte","derived"],[1739,"berlin","berlin-moabit","derived"],[1740,"berlin","berlin-neukölln","derived"],[1741,"berlin","berlin-pankow","derived"],[1742,"berlin","berlin-prenzlauer berg","derived"],[1743,"berlin","berlin-reinickendorf","derived"],[1744,"berlin","berlin-steglitz","derived"],[1745,"berlin","berlin-treptow","derived"],[1746,"berlin","berlin-wedding","derived"],[1747,"berlin","berlin-wilmersdorf","deri